WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.354 --> 00:00:03.506
- On this episode, I
talk about names of apps,

00:00:03.506 --> 00:00:06.274
listicles, and why nobody took over

00:00:06.274 --> 00:00:09.767
at Wine Library TV.

00:00:10.666 --> 00:00:12.076
You keep asking questions.

00:00:12.076 --> 00:00:15.025
I will continue to answer them.

00:00:19.565 --> 00:00:21.724
Hey everybody, this is Gary Vay-ner-chuk,

00:00:21.724 --> 00:00:23.744
and you're watching episode 30 of

00:00:23.744 --> 00:00:25.119
the #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:26.826
Fun fact about the number 30,

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:30.762
when I turned 30, I
freaked the (beeps) out

00:00:30.762 --> 00:00:32.539
and started Wine Library TV, and kind of

00:00:32.539 --> 00:00:34.280
changed the course of my life.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.013
I'm actually quite scared about 40.

00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:39.678
- [Voiceover] Maurizio asks, "Hi Gary,

00:00:39.678 --> 00:00:43.324
"what's your opinion on listicle sites?"

00:00:44.606 --> 00:00:46.922
- If you haven't noticed,
back in San Francisco

00:00:46.922 --> 00:00:48.959
for episode 30, excited about that.

00:00:48.959 --> 00:00:50.667
Listicles.

00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:52.373
You know, it's interesting.

00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:53.783
I have a very different view on this than

00:00:53.783 --> 00:00:55.455
a lot of my friends and contemporaries

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:57.266
because a lot of my
friends and contemporaries

00:00:57.266 --> 00:01:00.976
are journalists or have loved growing up

00:01:00.976 --> 00:01:03.030
reading the Wall Street
Journal or the New York Times

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.538
or the Post, or these kind of things.

00:01:05.538 --> 00:01:08.812
I think there's way too
much romance in journalism,

00:01:08.812 --> 00:01:10.013
and here's what I mean by that.

00:01:10.013 --> 00:01:12.504
I'm a huge fan of it because BuzzFeed

00:01:12.504 --> 00:01:14.750
and UpWorthy and companies of that nature,

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.309
and let's not forget the
USA Today really started

00:01:17.309 --> 00:01:20.183
infographics and listicles
in its modern sense,

00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:21.350
though listicles have been around

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.038
for 100 plus years.

00:01:23.038 --> 00:01:26.121
The people that are exploiting listicles

00:01:26.121 --> 00:01:30.265
and our worlds are being
overrun by 12 things

00:01:30.265 --> 00:01:33.417
a cat did while it ate
food, everybody who's

00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:35.508
complained about that needs to understand,

00:01:35.508 --> 00:01:37.962
this is the same debate
we had about reality TV,

00:01:37.962 --> 00:01:39.652
the same thing we had about the people

00:01:39.652 --> 00:01:42.054
that didn't like game
shows during the daytime,

00:01:42.054 --> 00:01:45.334
the same thing we are
about everything, meaning

00:01:48.104 --> 00:01:50.430
there's a huge misunderstanding

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:51.807
of how these things work.

00:01:51.807 --> 00:01:54.209
Here's my example, what do
I think about listicles?

00:01:54.209 --> 00:01:57.657
As a business? 24 months ago, phenomenal.

00:01:57.657 --> 00:01:58.947
You were going up in trend.

00:01:58.947 --> 00:02:01.071
Right now, super strong.

00:02:01.071 --> 00:02:03.508
24 months from now, solid.

00:02:03.508 --> 00:02:06.208
48 months from now, hmm.

00:02:06.208 --> 00:02:08.576
72 months from now, concerned.

00:02:08.576 --> 00:02:11.066
See what happens is we get these trends,

00:02:11.066 --> 00:02:13.104
they matter, people enjoy them, and then

00:02:13.104 --> 00:02:14.776
marketers ruin it.

00:02:14.776 --> 00:02:18.137
We love listicles 24
months ago as a big base,

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:19.878
then you had the cynics and the haters

00:02:19.878 --> 00:02:21.567
that started a little bit earlier,

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:24.319
and now what you're seeing
is, you're starting to have

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:26.007
a conversation of like, is it too much?

00:02:26.007 --> 00:02:27.436
Are we clicking as many?

00:02:27.436 --> 00:02:30.326
And the reaction of the market
is always what dictates.

00:02:30.326 --> 00:02:32.033
I love the customer.

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:33.478
I don't love anything else.

00:02:33.478 --> 00:02:35.724
So as long as the customer is enjoying it

00:02:35.724 --> 00:02:37.536
and they're clicking it, everyone says

00:02:37.536 --> 00:02:40.270
the Kardashians are crap, but
million of people watch it.

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:41.890
These things are subjective.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.353
Listicles are subjective.

00:02:43.353 --> 00:02:46.957
My POV on it, my subjectiveness on it

00:02:46.957 --> 00:02:49.900
is if people enjoy them, then
that's what it's going to be.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.234
And so for now and the next 36 months,

00:02:52.234 --> 00:02:54.044
I'm bullish on it as a business.

00:02:54.044 --> 00:02:56.639
As far as a consumer, I
don't consume anything,

00:02:56.639 --> 00:02:58.363
so it doesn't matter.

00:02:58.363 --> 00:03:00.839
- [Voiceover] Gabriel
ponders, "Gary, in the age

00:03:00.839 --> 00:03:05.210
"of social media, tweet, vine,
Instagram length limited,

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:08.397
"how does, should this affect a startup

00:03:08.397 --> 00:03:10.556
"in choosing a name?"

00:03:10.556 --> 00:03:11.845
- Gabriel, this is a great question.

00:03:11.845 --> 00:03:13.202
I understand what you're saying, right,

00:03:13.202 --> 00:03:16.006
Twitter, tweet, you know,
kind of the short form,

00:03:16.006 --> 00:03:20.342
you know I actually think
it's an interesting question.

00:03:20.342 --> 00:03:21.770
The reason I decided to choose it is,

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:23.686
a name is made.

00:03:23.686 --> 00:03:25.699
Meaning, when everybody's sitting around,

00:03:25.699 --> 00:03:27.353
I have friends who sit
around and think about

00:03:27.353 --> 00:03:30.504
picking the name of the
startup for 900 years.

00:03:30.504 --> 00:03:33.447
And I tell them, what did
Google mean to anybody?

00:03:33.447 --> 00:03:35.033
What did Facebook mean to anybody

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:37.105
outside of people that were
in the Harvard community

00:03:37.105 --> 00:03:38.777
that knew what the facebook was?

00:03:38.777 --> 00:03:40.518
None of these words mean anything

00:03:40.518 --> 00:03:41.754
until there's something, right?

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:44.175
People email me like, uh
I don't like my last name,

00:03:44.175 --> 00:03:46.909
like you're emailing Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:49.069
I mean, Vaynerchuk sucks.

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:50.635
Let's call it what it is, right?

00:03:50.635 --> 00:03:52.395
And so now a bunch of you are gonna leave

00:03:52.395 --> 00:03:53.927
in the comments. No no
no, it's kind of unique,

00:03:53.927 --> 00:03:55.714
it's good. I get it.

00:03:55.714 --> 00:03:58.907
But it's only good because
I made something out of it.

00:03:58.907 --> 00:04:01.466
And so, what does it mean?

00:04:01.466 --> 00:04:02.895
It means nothing to me.

00:04:02.895 --> 00:04:04.672
If you went with a long-winded startup,

00:04:04.672 --> 00:04:05.873
you could get abbreviations.

00:04:05.873 --> 00:04:06.987
I actually think that's a new trend.

00:04:06.987 --> 00:04:08.294
Somebody's gonna come out with a company

00:04:08.294 --> 00:04:10.435
called like Copperhound Thompson,

00:04:10.435 --> 00:04:12.978
but everybody's gonna call it CHT, right?

00:04:12.978 --> 00:04:16.461
And so like in a world where
we're short-forming everything,

00:04:16.461 --> 00:04:19.369
people call me V-chuk
because they don't want to

00:04:19.369 --> 00:04:20.623
say Vaynerchuk and that became

00:04:20.623 --> 00:04:22.451
my slang last name to my
friends in high school

00:04:22.451 --> 00:04:25.429
and so we will evolve our
name into our convenience

00:04:25.429 --> 00:04:27.223
if it brings value.

00:04:27.223 --> 00:04:28.633
So stop worrying about the name,

00:04:28.633 --> 00:04:32.574
and start worrying about the product.

00:04:32.574 --> 00:04:34.514
- [Voiceover] Noble Rot Society.

00:04:34.514 --> 00:04:37.436
(background murmuring)

00:04:37.436 --> 00:04:40.005
(laughter)

00:04:42.123 --> 00:04:43.331
- Right?

00:04:44.544 --> 00:04:46.233
- [Voiceover] I haven't
written any blog posts.

00:04:46.233 --> 00:04:47.905
- I highly recommend it
if your name's available

00:04:47.905 --> 00:04:50.708
or if you own your dot com,
to just do it under that.

00:04:50.708 --> 00:04:51.421
- [Voiceover] I do too.

00:04:51.421 --> 00:04:52.109
- Cool.

00:04:52.109 --> 00:04:54.043
- [Voiceover] Noble Rot Society asks,

00:04:54.043 --> 00:04:56.776
"Gary, when you left Wine Library

00:04:56.776 --> 00:04:59.528
"to start VaynerMedia, was
it a conscious decision

00:04:59.528 --> 00:05:03.340
"not to have someone take
over Wine Library TV?"

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:04.820
- This is a great question.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:06.090
People have asked me this question a lot,

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:08.128
and I wanna interject some depth,

00:05:08.128 --> 00:05:09.747
like last couple questions,
last couple episodes

00:05:09.747 --> 00:05:11.279
with some of this kind of fun stuff.

00:05:11.279 --> 00:05:13.039
And insight that I've never talked about.

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:15.180
You know, I don't know if
it was a conscious decision.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.644
Nobody really raised their hand,

00:05:16.644 --> 00:05:17.757
nobody wanted that spot.

00:05:17.757 --> 00:05:19.639
It's kind of like my
speaking career right now,

00:05:19.639 --> 00:05:21.084
knock on wood I'm doing so well,

00:05:21.084 --> 00:05:23.122
the bad part is I always
have to speak last

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:26.250
because nobody wants to go next.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.764
I don't think it was the
right or obvious choice

00:05:28.764 --> 00:05:31.620
internally at Wine Library to jump on it,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:34.856
Ian, Brandon, you know all
these great people internally

00:05:34.856 --> 00:05:38.669
just didn't' wanna have
the kind of, you know,

00:05:38.669 --> 00:05:41.559
not responsibility, look it
takes a certain personality

00:05:41.559 --> 00:05:42.639
to put yourself out there.

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:44.224
We've talked about the past, my blog post

00:05:44.224 --> 00:05:46.227
about this the other day did really well.

00:05:46.227 --> 00:05:48.807
You can link that, Stunwin.

00:05:48.807 --> 00:05:52.548
So it wasn't a conscious
choice, it was the right choice.

00:05:52.548 --> 00:05:54.202
If that person existed in the building,

00:05:54.202 --> 00:05:56.919
maybe it would have
continued in that manner,

00:05:56.919 --> 00:05:59.565
but I think one thing that
I'm really focused on,

00:05:59.565 --> 00:06:01.394
why I took this question to interweave it

00:06:01.394 --> 00:06:02.857
to everybody else while answering it

00:06:02.857 --> 00:06:06.828
is I think you have to assess.

00:06:06.828 --> 00:06:09.335
Like you always have to assess.

00:06:09.335 --> 00:06:11.564
And I think way too
many people come in with

00:06:11.564 --> 00:06:13.776
what they want it to be, the notion of,

00:06:13.776 --> 00:06:15.499
somebody will take over this show,

00:06:15.499 --> 00:06:16.806
and then you're forcing someone

00:06:16.806 --> 00:06:18.582
to sit in the hotseat, and then you really

00:06:18.582 --> 00:06:19.957
lose the lustre of it.

00:06:19.957 --> 00:06:22.901
Not to mention, I may want to jump back

00:06:22.901 --> 00:06:24.224
in that seat one day.

00:06:24.224 --> 00:06:25.249
(bell dings)

00:06:25.249 --> 00:06:28.071
Question of the day.

00:06:28.071 --> 00:06:29.359
Where do I want to, San Francisco,

00:06:29.359 --> 00:06:31.204
I'm in San Francisco, I
should ask a San Francisco,

00:06:31.204 --> 00:06:34.374
what is your favorite
thing about San Francisco?

00:06:34.374 --> 00:06:36.168
And remember, you guys are
letting me down on this

00:06:36.168 --> 00:06:37.579
'cause you're so focused
on answering questions.

00:06:37.579 --> 00:06:38.902
I want critiques.

00:06:38.902 --> 00:06:40.191
Now that you've had two of them,

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:42.716
give me the overall feel of
these five-minute episodes.

00:06:42.716 --> 00:06:44.091
You keep asking questions!

00:06:44.091 --> 00:06:45.327
Oh!

